1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,546
Here's how that happens. Version 1.2.0
is released on a Monday morning.

2
00:00:09,586 --> 00:00:14,273
An hour later someone finds a bug
in a limit. The fix is one line.

3
00:00:14,313 --> 00:00:20,076
It lands on main. And then a well-meaning
engineer thinks: the release is only an

4
00:00:20,116 --> 00:00:25,878
hour old, hardly anyone has it, let me
correct the tag so that 1.2.0 includes the

5
00:00:25,918 --> 00:00:28,456
fix. Almost everyone has thought it.

6
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:32,637
Git refuses twice. The engineer
adds a force flag twice.

7
00:00:32,677 --> 00:00:38,387
From that moment two different pieces of
code are called 1.2.0, and no command that

8
00:00:38,427 --> 00:00:43,445
any of your colleagues runs in the
normal course of a day will tell them.

9
00:00:43,485 --> 00:00:46,286
So which one is 1.2.0? Keep the question.

10
00:00:48,126 --> 00:00:51,988
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:52,028 --> 00:00:57,298
In the previous video you learned three
facts. A tag is a ref, a name that holds an

12
00:00:57,338 --> 00:01:02,415
object ID, and every clone holds it
separately. A push sends it only when asked.

13
00:01:02,755 --> 00:01:07,550
And a fetch never updates a tag you already
have. Each fact was harmless on its own.

14
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:12,154
Today you put them together and reproduce
the incident between three clones: the

15
00:01:12,194 --> 00:01:16,816
releaser, a colleague who fetched before
the move, and a CI machine, an automated

16
00:01:16,856 --> 00:01:18,831
build server, that cloned after it.

17
00:01:19,171 --> 00:01:23,548
Then you detect the disagreement, repair it
with the lowest-risk action, and look at

18
00:01:23,588 --> 00:01:27,965
prevention on the server, including two
settings whose names suggest that they help,

19
00:01:28,005 --> 00:01:28,491
and don't.

20
00:01:30,331 --> 00:01:34,297
reproduce a moved tag between two
clones and say who has which version;

21
00:01:34,637 --> 00:01:39,304
explain why git fetch keeps the
old tag and what --force changes;

22
00:01:39,644 --> 00:01:43,594
detect the disagreement by comparing
local tags with the server;

23
00:01:43,934 --> 00:01:48,273
repair it with the lowest-risk action
and say why a new version number is that

24
00:01:48,313 --> 00:01:48,666
action;

25
00:01:49,006 --> 00:01:53,751
name the server-side settings that refuse
a tag update on a plain Git server.

26
00:01:55,591 --> 00:02:01,043
In one sentence: every clone keeps its own
copy of each tag and never updates it by

27
00:02:01,083 --> 00:02:05,543
itself. So moving a tag on the server
doesn't move it anywhere else.

28
00:02:05,583 --> 00:02:10,836
It creates two things with one name. On
screen, the server and Asha's clone from

29
00:02:10,876 --> 00:02:15,468
today's demo: the fix lands on main,
and then v1.2.0 is moved onto it.

30
00:02:15,808 --> 00:02:20,743
Why does Git behave this way? Because
a tag name is a promise about content.

31
00:02:20,783 --> 00:02:25,915
The manual says that Git "does not (and
it should not) change tags behind users

32
00:02:25,955 --> 00:02:30,825
back". A fetch creates missing tags and
refuses to overwrite existing ones.

33
00:02:30,865 --> 00:02:34,491
So in Asha's clone a pull
moves main, and her tag stays.

34
00:02:34,531 --> 00:02:37,698
A plain fetch doesn't even
report the difference.

35
00:02:38,038 --> 00:02:43,499
What moving a tag takes. Locally, git tag
-f. That is 🟡 CAUTION: it rewrites the

36
00:02:43,539 --> 00:02:48,175
ref, the old tag object becomes
unreachable, and there is no reflog.

37
00:02:48,215 --> 00:02:53,539
Git prints the old ID once, "Updated tag
(was ...)", and that line is the only

38
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:58,696
record. On the server, git push --force
origin <tag>, which is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

39
00:02:59,036 --> 00:03:04,077
How to detect it. Ask the server for the
peeled tag with git ls-remote and compare

40
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:07,671
with your own git rev-parse of
the tag peeled to a commit.

41
00:03:07,711 --> 00:03:11,327
Peeled means followed through
the tag object to its commit.

42
00:03:11,367 --> 00:03:15,850
git fetch --tags also reports the
clash, as "would clobber existing tag".

43
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:19,305
How to repair it. The
textbook's fix has two halves.

44
00:03:19,345 --> 00:03:24,158
Put the tag back where it was published.
And release the new content under a new

45
00:03:24,198 --> 00:03:24,461
name.

46
00:03:24,801 --> 00:03:30,172
Why is a new version number the low-risk
action? Because it changes nothing that

47
00:03:30,212 --> 00:03:35,515
anyone already has. Every clone, every
cache and every built artifact that says

48
00:03:35,555 --> 00:03:41,129
1.2.0 becomes right again, and the fix is
available as 1.2.1 to anyone who asks for

49
00:03:41,169 --> 00:03:46,472
it. Any other repair requires reaching
every machine that ever fetched the tag.

50
00:03:46,812 --> 00:03:52,188
Clones that picked up the moved tag in
between must be corrected by hand, with git

51
00:03:52,228 --> 00:03:57,735
fetch --tags --force. That is 🟡 CAUTION: it
overwrites local tags with the server's.

52
00:03:58,075 --> 00:04:03,460
How to prevent it. On the server. On a plain
Git server, with a hook, a program Git

53
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:06,924
runs at a fixed point. On
GitHub, with a tag ruleset.

54
00:04:06,964 --> 00:04:12,414
And in your deployment process: deploy by
commit ID or image digest, not by tag name

55
00:04:12,454 --> 00:04:12,806
alone.

56
00:04:13,146 --> 00:04:17,704
When is a forced tag push appropriate at
all? The safety table gives one case:

57
00:04:17,744 --> 00:04:22,655
putting a tag back at its published position.
A name that is meant to move should be

58
00:04:22,695 --> 00:04:23,185
a branch.

59
00:04:25,025 --> 00:04:30,041
Use the certificates from the last video:
an annotated tag is a certificate that

60
00:04:30,081 --> 00:04:35,286
cites a page of the ledger. Every clone has
its own copy of the certificate "v1.2.0

61
00:04:35,326 --> 00:04:40,531
cites page d20ef7a". Moving the tag on the
server writes a new certificate with the

62
00:04:40,571 --> 00:04:45,397
same title that cites a different page,
and files it at the front desk of one

63
00:04:45,437 --> 00:04:45,839
office.

64
00:04:46,179 --> 00:04:50,198
Offices that already hold a certificate
with that title keep theirs.

65
00:04:50,238 --> 00:04:55,153
That's a policy, not an oversight: a clerk
who silently replaced certificates would

66
00:04:55,193 --> 00:04:59,929
make every certificate worthless. Offices
that open after the change get the new

67
00:04:59,969 --> 00:05:02,019
one. Nobody is told that two exist.

68
00:05:02,359 --> 00:05:07,038
Where the model breaks: paper certificates
could be compared by a date stamp.

69
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:12,187
Two tag objects with the same name are
distinguished only by their object IDs and by

70
00:05:12,227 --> 00:05:16,968
the commit each one peels to. So the
comparison you run is always between IDs.

71
00:05:18,808 --> 00:05:24,744
Try it now, on paper. Pause me for thirty
seconds and draw three boxes: the server,

72
00:05:24,784 --> 00:05:30,792
Asha's clone, and a CI clone made after the
move. Your answer for each: which commit

73
00:05:30,832 --> 00:05:31,512
is v1.2.0?

74
00:05:36,352 --> 00:05:42,297
Three repositories, one tag name, two
commits. Asha's clone has the commit that was

75
00:05:42,337 --> 00:05:47,344
released. The CI clone has the commit
that the server now calls 1.2.0.

76
00:05:47,384 --> 00:05:50,301
Both will build something labelled 1.2.0.

77
00:05:52,141 --> 00:05:57,409
v1.2.0 is the tag object 5b231b5,
and it peels to commit d20ef7a.

78
00:05:57,449 --> 00:06:03,289
You and Asha both have it. Now the
fix lands and the tag is "corrected".

79
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:08,804
Count the refusals. git tag needed
-f and printed "(was 5b231b5)".

80
00:06:08,844 --> 00:06:14,018
The push was rejected: "already
exists". Only --force got through.

81
00:06:14,058 --> 00:06:16,625
Two safety nets, both overridden.

82
00:06:16,965 --> 00:06:17,964
Now Asha pulls.

83
00:06:20,304 --> 00:06:25,823
Ask the audience: after this pull, what
does Asha's clone report for v1.2.0?

84
00:06:25,863 --> 00:06:27,798
Her main will have the fix.

85
00:06:28,138 --> 00:06:35,542
Her main is at 2518733, with the fix. Her
v1.2.0 is where it always was, on d20ef7a.

86
00:06:35,582 --> 00:06:42,631
The pull printed nothing about the tag.
A machine that clones now gets the other

87
00:06:42,671 --> 00:06:42,986
one.

88
00:06:49,126 --> 00:06:57,390
In the CI clone, v1.2.0 decorates 2518733,
and the limits file at that tag says

89
00:06:57,430 --> 00:07:03,276
max_tokens: 4096. In Asha's
clone the same tag has 2048.

90
00:07:03,316 --> 00:07:06,534
Both builds are labelled 1.2.0.

91
00:07:06,874 --> 00:07:08,592
Detect it, from Asha's clone.

92
00:07:08,932 --> 00:07:17,010
The server says 2518733. This clone says
d20ef7a. And git fetch --tags rejects the

93
00:07:17,050 --> 00:07:20,673
update: "would clobber existing tag".

94
00:07:21,013 --> 00:07:26,165
Repair it. Asha's clone still has the
original tag object, so she can restore it.

95
00:07:26,205 --> 00:07:29,691
This is the one appropriate
use of the forced tag push.

96
00:07:37,531 --> 00:07:42,875
The first command runs in Asha's clone
and puts 5b231b5 back on the server.

97
00:07:42,915 --> 00:07:48,691
The rest run in yours: you take the server's
tag by force, and release the fix as

98
00:07:48,731 --> 00:07:54,362
v1.2.1. If nobody had kept the original
ref, the tag object is usually still in

99
00:07:54,402 --> 00:07:59,531
someone's object database as a "dangling
tag". The lab does that search.

100
00:07:59,871 --> 00:08:05,183
Is everyone right now? Predict what the
CI clone shows after an ordinary fetch.

101
00:08:05,223 --> 00:08:07,012
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

102
00:08:18,052 --> 00:08:23,820
After the plain fetch, the CI clone has
v1.2.1 and v1.2.0 on the same commit.

103
00:08:23,860 --> 00:08:28,572
It received the new tag and kept
its wrong copy of the old one.

104
00:08:28,612 --> 00:08:34,909
Only git fetch --tags --force corrects it.
Every clone and cache that fetched in the

105
00:08:34,949 --> 00:08:37,322
bad interval needs this by hand.

106
00:08:37,662 --> 00:08:41,476
Prevention. Two settings of plain
Git sound as if they would help.

107
00:08:41,816 --> 00:08:47,279
Quick quiz, two options. With both settings
on, the forced tag push is refused.

108
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:49,647
Or it's accepted. Say it out loud.

109
00:08:54,487 --> 00:08:59,284
Accepted, exit status 0. If you said
refused, the names do suggest it.

110
00:08:59,324 --> 00:09:04,742
The textbook cites the source: in Git
2.55.0 the code behind these two settings

111
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:10,338
applies them only to refs under refs/heads/,
and the manual's wording doesn't say

112
00:09:10,378 --> 00:09:14,207
so. On a plain Git server,
tags are protected by a hook.

113
00:09:17,547 --> 00:09:23,127
An update hook is called once per ref with
the ref name, the old ID and the new ID.

114
00:09:23,167 --> 00:09:28,746
For a ref under refs/tags/v, if the old ID
isn't all zeros, the tag exists already,

115
00:09:28,786 --> 00:09:32,199
and the hook refuses. A
release tag may be created.

116
00:09:32,239 --> 00:09:34,298
It may not be moved or deleted.

117
00:09:36,138 --> 00:09:37,786
Five mistakes to watch for.

118
00:09:38,126 --> 00:09:41,306
"Correcting" a release tag
an hour after publishing it.

119
00:09:41,346 --> 00:09:46,166
Root cause: every clone that fetched keeps
its copy, so the move creates two things

120
00:09:46,206 --> 00:09:48,391
with one name instead of changing one.

121
00:09:48,731 --> 00:09:52,003
Believing that a pull or fetch
brings the corrected tag.

122
00:09:52,043 --> 00:09:56,735
Root cause: fetch creates missing tags
and refuses to overwrite existing ones; a

123
00:09:56,775 --> 00:09:59,573
plain fetch does not even
report the difference.

124
00:09:59,913 --> 00:10:04,240
Relying on receive.denyNonFastForwards
to protect tags.

125
00:10:04,280 --> 00:10:10,909
Root cause: in Git 2.55.0 the code applies
it, and receive.denyDeletes, only to refs

126
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:12,338
under refs/heads/.

127
00:10:12,678 --> 00:10:17,269
Repairing by moving the tag a third
time. Root cause: each move adds another

128
00:10:17,309 --> 00:10:22,205
interval in which clones captured a different
value; the only stable value is the

129
00:10:22,245 --> 00:10:23,728
one originally published.

130
00:10:24,068 --> 00:10:29,522
Deploying by tag name alone. Root cause:
a tag is a mutable name held by someone

131
00:10:29,562 --> 00:10:33,024
else; the commit ID or image
digest is the content.

132
00:10:34,864 --> 00:10:39,295
Now, out of the lab. The CTO's question
is also a supply-chain question.

133
00:10:39,335 --> 00:10:44,138
The research report behind this course
records that third-party GitHub Actions

134
00:10:44,178 --> 00:10:48,981
referenced by tag were repointed in real
attacks, which is why the course pins

135
00:10:49,021 --> 00:10:54,196
actions by full commit ID. The mechanism is
exactly today's: a tag is a mutable name

136
00:10:54,236 --> 00:10:55,500
held by someone else.

137
00:10:55,840 --> 00:10:59,408
A model-serving team applies
the lesson to its own releases.

138
00:10:59,448 --> 00:11:04,400
The build writes the full commit ID and the
image digest into the deployment record

139
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:09,452
next to the tag name. The production deploy
job refuses to proceed if the tag on the

140
00:11:09,492 --> 00:11:12,038
server doesn't peel to
the recorded commit.

141
00:11:12,378 --> 00:11:17,403
And the repository has a rule that
existing release tags can't be updated or

142
00:11:17,443 --> 00:11:23,000
deleted. When a bug is found an hour after
a release, the conversation is short: the

143
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:26,532
fix is the next patch version.
So which one is 1.2.0?

144
00:11:26,572 --> 00:11:28,132
The one published first.

