identify what they deploy.

161
00:12:47,193 --> 00:12:50,121
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

162
00:12:50,461 --> 00:12:54,942
A tag exists once per clone; moving
it on the server changes one copy.

163
00:12:55,282 --> 00:13:00,691
Fetch creates missing tags and refuses to
overwrite existing ones, so old clones and

164
00:13:00,731 --> 00:13:02,572
new clones disagree silently.

165
00:13:02,912 --> 00:13:07,189
I detect a moved tag by comparing
the server's peeled tag with mine.

166
00:13:07,529 --> 00:13:13,203
The repair is to restore the published
tag and ship the change as a new version;

167
00:13:13,243 --> 00:13:16,131
stragglers need git fetch --tags --force.

168
00:13:16,471 --> 00:13:21,404
Tag immutability is enforced on the server:
by a hook on plain Git, by a ruleset on

169
00:13:21,444 --> 00:13:21,823
GitHub.

